BandC wrote:

> 1) When you are bitten by werejackal for example, are you screwed for
> life? Are there things you can do to cure yourself or prevent being
> bitten short of running away or teleporting?

There is a food item that cures you. You'll recognise it when you see
it. Alternatively, pray.

> 2) Use of sinks and fountains: Should I drink? Not drink? What are
> benefits and disadvantages? Does it help at all with hunger? What
> should I dip?

The majority in this newsgroup advises not to dring from sinks or
fountains, but a lot of people think otherwise. You should probably wait
until you learn to be a better player.

Some people like quaffing with a starting character, and take it for
granted that this might kill lots of those.

Benefits and disadvantages vary wildly: from getting a wish to being
surrounded by hostile demons or your inventory being cursed partly...

> 3) Praying: I know it helps with hunger. Though when I prayed a second
> time the god was displeased. Is praying a last resort option? How does
> it work? What does it help with?

You might want to pray a little as possible. Every time you pray, a
counter is set to a random value (ranging from 0 to 3500, approximately,
IIRC). Every turn, this counter is reduced by one, and when it reaches 0
once more, you may pray again.

In my experience, the "prayer timeout", as it is called here, is rarely
more than 800 or so. Still, I consider it wise to use your prayers only
when you are in deep sh*t. Your god may pull you out.

Prayer may solve lots of problems, varying from filling your tummy to
curing sickness or healing HP loss. The latter, most specifically, will
only work when you're at or lower than 5 HP or 1/7th of your total HP.

> 4) Altars: I know it lets you identify cursed/blessed items. Any other
> uses? Obviously type of altar (lawful, etc.) matters. How so?

Praying works differently when your standing at an altar. You may offer
fresh corpses at altars, which may result in the altar being converted
to your own god, or, if the altar was already your god's, in your god
granting you favours or artefacts.

> 5) Being threatened: What does it mean? This happens sometimes when I
> read spellbooks. Also, is it bad to try to read books you cannot
> easily comprehend (game warning)?

When you try to read a difficult spellbook, several bad things may
happen. You will be paralysed, may be teleported or poisoned, and the
spellbook may crumble to dust.

The game warning that a spell is too difficult to comprehend will only
appear when you're playing a wizard; other classes get no warning, and
some of the above bad effects may occur.

> 6) Scrolls, spellbooks, potions: The names of these are randomized
> every game. Correct? So I've been just using/reading these to figure
> out what they do every game. Obviously, I end up not using most of
> them because I'm afraid of negative effects. Are there ways of
> identifying these short of scroll of identifications (which needs to
